# Explore Tropical Social Aggregation

Begin with the central surprise: preserving tropical minima and common translations does **not** force dictatorship. The binary minimum combines two voters and satisfies both laws.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The widget shows the active coordinate changing across the equality wall. For a reproducible numerical tour—including normalization, non-dictatorship witnesses, dependence tests, and concavity—run the complete demonstration:

{{demo:0}}

## The algebraic laws

A profile is a real score vector $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$. Coordinatewise tropical addition is $x\wedge y$, and common scalar translation is $x+c\mathbf 1$. A weakly tropical-linear score satisfies $f(x\wedge y)=\min(f(x),f(y))$ and $f(x+c\mathbf1)=f(x)+c$.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why binary minimum preserves tropical addition</summary>
For two voters, both $m(x\wedge y)$ and $\min(m(x),m(y))$ are simply the minimum of the same four values $x_1,x_2,y_1,y_2$. Associativity and commutativity of minimum make the two parenthesizations equal.
</details>

## Which coalitions carry all information?

A coalition is sufficient when changing every score outside it leaves the output fixed. Explore the difference between a one-voter projection and a two-voter minimum.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the finite ultrafilter argument</summary>
An ultrafilter chooses one side of every partition and is closed under finite intersections. On a finite electorate, intersecting all decisive coalitions leaves a nonempty set. It can contain only one voter; otherwise the ultrafilter dichotomy separates two of its members. Thus every decisive coalition contains one unique voter.
</details>

## From decisive voter to projection

Once the decisive voter is $d$, compare any profile $x$ with the constant profile $x_d\mathbf1$. They agree at $d$, so dependence gives equal outputs. Translation equivariance and normalization evaluate the constant profile as $x_d$. This is the finite tropical Arrow theorem.

{{algorithm:2}}

## Polyhedral geometry and concavity

The graph of $\min(x_1,x_2)$ consists of two planes meeting on $x_1=x_2$. These are chambers of decisiveness.

{{visualization:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

Concavity says $m((1-t)x+ty)\ge(1-t)m(x)+tm(y)$. Explore its gap dynamically:

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the concavity proof</summary>
For each coordinate $j$, $x_j\ge m(x)$ and $y_j\ge m(y)$. Multiplying by the nonnegative weights $1-t$ and $t$, then adding, shows every mixed coordinate is at least $(1-t)m(x)+tm(y)$. Their minimum has the same lower bound.
</details>

## Continue learning

Background resources include [tropical geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_geometry), [ultrafilters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultrafilter), [social choice theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_choice_theory), and [concave functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concave_function). The open frontier is to classify every finite min-plus aggregator and connect its support to a polyhedral complex of decisive coalitions.
